Rin_2_7 photo_2_7 horiz_2_7 10000
Rlat_2_7_d horiz_2_7 horiz_3_7 15000
Rin_3_3 photo_3_3 horiz_3_3 10000
Rlat_3_3_r horiz_3_3 horiz_3_4 15000
Rlat_3_3_d horiz_3_3 horiz_4_3 15000
Rin_3_4 photo_3_4 horiz_3_4 10000
Rlat_3_4_r horiz_3_4 horiz_3_5 15000
Rlat_3_4_d horiz_3_4 horiz_4_4 15000
Rin_3_5 photo_3_5 horiz_3_5 10000
Rlat_3_5_r horiz_3_5 horiz_3_6 15000
Rlat_3_5_d horiz_3_5 horiz_4_5 15000
Rin_3_6 photo_3_6 horiz_3_6 10000
Rlat_3_6_r horiz_3_6 horiz_3_7 15000
Rlat_3_6_d horiz_3_6 horiz_4_6 15000
Rin_3_7 photo_3_7 horiz_3_7 10000
Rlat_3_7_d horiz_3_7 horiz_4_7 15000
Rin_4_3 photo_4_3 horiz_4_3 10000
Rlat_4_3_r horiz_4_3 horiz_4_4 15000
Rlat_4_3_d horiz_4_3 horiz_5_3 15000
Rin_4_4 photo_4_4 horiz_4_4 10000
Rlat_4_4_r horiz_4_4 horiz_4_5 15000
Rlat_4_4_d horiz_4_4 horiz_5_4 15000
Rin_4_5 photo_4_5 horiz_4_5 10000
Rlat_4_5_r horiz_4_5 horiz_4_6 15000
Rlat_4_5_d horiz_4_5 horiz_5_5 15000
Rin_4_6 photo_4_6 horiz_4_6 10000
Rlat_4_6_r horiz_4_6 horiz_4_7 15000
Rlat_4_6_d horiz_4_6 horiz_5_6 15000
Rin_4_7 photo_4_7 horiz_4_7 10000
Rlat_4_7_d horiz_4_7 horiz_5_7 15000
Rin_5_3 photo_5_3 horiz_5_3 10000
Rlat_5_3_r horiz_5_3 horiz_5_4 15000
Rin_5_4 photo_5_4 horiz_5_4 10000
Rlat_5_4_r horiz_5_4 horiz_5_5 15000
Rin_5_5 photo_5_5 horiz_5_5 10000
Rlat_5_5_r horiz_5_5 horiz_5_6 15000
Rin_5_6 photo_5_6 horiz_5_6 10000
Rlat_5_6_r horiz_5_6 horiz_5_7 15000
Rin_5_7 photo_5_7 horiz_5_7 10000

* Center-surround output at the target cell's real receptive-field
* location (row=3, col=5, from Phase 1's STA peak pixel).
Bcs centersurround 0 V=4*(V(photo_3_5)-V(horiz_3_5))

* Temporal dynamics: simple RC low-pass (bipolar-cell integration delay).
Rtau centersurround bipolar 30000
Ctau bipolar 0 1e-06

* Spike generation: leaky integrate-and-fire (comparator + reset switch,
* the standard SPICE trick for I&F neurons -- there's no built-in primitive).
Rmemin bipolar vmem 100000
Rleak vmem 0 2e+06
Cmem vmem 0 3e-07 IC=0
* A hard ternary comparator here (0 or 3.3V, instantaneously) was too
* numerically stiff for the solver to converge through -- it kept stalling at
* one timestep and never advancing (verified directly: 80 output rows, most
* with an IDENTICAL repeated timestamp, far short of the requested duration).
* A steep but smooth (differentiable) tanh sigmoid gives the same effective
* comparator behavior while staying solvable. Gain matters a lot here: an
* earlier version used gain 200, which is so steep that ngspice's local-
* truncation-error estimator collapsed the timestep near every threshold
* approach (measured directly: 525,632 internal steps for what should be
* ~80, a 15x slowdown). Gain 20 is still a sharp, effectively-binary
* comparator but stays numerically solvable.
Bspike spike 0 V=1.65*(1+tanh((V(vmem)-0.15)*20))
Sreset vmem 0 spike 0 SWRESET
* The switch's control node is the SPIKE signal (0..V_SPIKE), not vmem, so
* its Vt must be calibrated to that scale -- a real bug found here: an
* earlier version used Vt=V_THRESH/2 (0.075), a threshold meant for the
* ~0.1V-scale membrane voltage, on a signal that actually swings to 3.3V.
* That let the switch fire (and clamp vmem toward 0) the instant vmem got
* anywhere near threshold, long before the comparator actually went high --
* which is why vmem never rose past ~0.06V and no spikes were ever produced.
* Also softened Ron:Roff from 20,000:1 to 500:1 -- the original ratio was
* part of the same convergence problem.
.model SWRESET SW(Ron=200 Roff=100k Vt=1.65 Vh=0.66)

* Convergence aids for this stiff switched/nonlinear circuit -- reltol
* loosened from the 0.001 default (fine for this qualitative demo, not
* a precision analog design), gmin raised slightly, iteration limit raised.
.options reltol=0.01 gmin=1e-9 itl4=500

.tran 1.041667e-03 8.333333e-01 0 8.333333e-03 uic

.control
run
wrdata circuit_out.txt v(centersurround) v(bipolar) v(vmem) v(spike)
quit
.endc
.end